WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.450
In 2001, Russia asked a UN commission
for more of the Arctic seabed.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.470
Norway followed in 2006.

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.830
Denmark, for northern Greenland, in 2014.

4
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.890
Canada in 2019.

5
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:17.130
Above that seabed, the summer
ice has been shrinking.

6
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.930
The record low, in 2012, was
3.39 million square kilometres.

7
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.530
On 12 September 2026, the minimum
was a preliminary 4.6 million,

8
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.930
tied for tenth-lowest
in the satellite record.

9
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.930
So why does shrinking sea ice
make that seabed worth claiming?

10
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.110
In this mission, you'll test whether
summer sea-ice loss has passed a point of

11
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.430
no return.

12
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.710
Then you'll weigh who manages the Arctic,

13
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.030
and who lives with the change.

14
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.330
Your mission: explain why shrinking sea
ice makes Arctic seabed worth claiming.
